Model a Shrink Wrapper
Model a Shrink Wrapper
You can use the Turnplate to model a shrink wrapper. In our example we apply an overwrap around the
parts on our pallet to stabilize the load.
In this sample model the SourceParts creates nine parts. It moves them onto Conveyor1, which feeds
them to the Turnplate
 . The Turnplate turns them by 90 degrees to the right. The successor line moves
the parts onto the ParallelStation 
 , which processes them for two minutes. It then moves them on
to the TransferStation, which loads them onto a pallet. The ConveyorPallet moves the pallet back to the
Turnplate, which shrink wraps it, and moves it on to shipping, i.e., to the Drain.
To create our simulation model, proceed as follows:
• Configure the Source and the Feeder Line
• Configure the Turnplate
• Configure the Conveyor Which Transfers the Parts
• Configure the Stations Which Handle the Pallet
• Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
The finished simulation model looks like this in 2D:
Model a Shrink Wrapper
10-730 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Align and Shrink-Wrap Parts with the Turnplate
Configure the Source and the Feeder Line
Insert and configure the Source. As we want it to produce nine parts only, we selected Time of Creation >
Number Adjustable and entered 9 as the Amount. We then selected the part MyPart, which we created in
the folder UserObjects.
Configure the Source and the Feeder Line
Plant Simulation Help 10-731
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Next, insert the feeder line that moves the part on to the Turnplate. You can use the Conveyor with its
default settings.
Back to Model a Shrink Wrapper
Configure the Turnplate
Insert the Turnplate and configure it. The Turnplate has two tasks in our example.
• It rotates the parts by 90 degrees to the right and then moves them on to Conveyor1.
• It rotates the pallet after the parts have been stacked onto it to simulate the shrink wrapper.
To accomplish this, we have to use a method:
• Select the Strategy > Method.
• Specify the name of the Strategy Method and program the actions it has to execute.
We decided to use a user-defined attribute of data type method. We right-clicked in the text box
Strategy Method and selected Create Control. Plant Simulation then entered self.OnStrategy and
opened the method.
Configure the Turnplate
10-732 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

We typed in the following source code, which first sets the rotation angle of the part itself and then
of the pallet after the parts have been loaded onto it. The leading sign in the instruction -(4 *
360) designates the direction in which the Turnplate rotates; + stands for clockwise, - stands for
counterclockwise. Our example rotates the pallet four times by 360 degrees counterclockwise.
var rotAngle: integer
if @.typeOf(.UserObjects.MyPart)           // rotates the part
   rotAngle := 90
else
   if @.typeOf(.UserObjects.MyPallet)      // rotates the pallet
       rotAngle := -(4 * 360)              // minus (-) designates 
counterclockwise rotation
   end
end
?.rotatePart(rotAngle)
Back to Model a Shrink Wrapper
Configure the Conveyor Which Transfers the Parts
Insert and configure the Conveyor which receives the parts from the Turnplate.
All you have to do here is to define the sensor which triggers moving the parts to the ParallelStation.
• Click the tab Controls.
Configure the Conveyor Which Transfers the Parts
Plant Simulation Help 10-733
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Click 
  and define a length-oriented sensor that is front triggered.
• Right-click into the text box Control and select Create Control.
• Copy the following source code and paste it. It determines how to move on the part or the pallet.
param sensorID: integer, front: boolean
if @.typeOf(.UserObjects.MyPart)
   @.move(ParallelStation)
end
Back to Model a Shrink Wrapper
Configure the Conveyor Which Transfers the Parts
10-734 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Configure the Stations Which Handle the Pallet
Finally, insert and configure the stations which create and handle the pallet onto which the parts will be
loaded.
• First, insert and configure the Source which produces the pallet. To produce a single pallet after 10
minutes, select Time of Creation > Number Adjustable and enter an Amount of 1. Enter 10:00 as the
Creation Times.
Then, select MyPallet as the MU to be produced.
• Next, insert the ParallelStation onto which Conveyor1 moves the parts. You do not have to change any
of its settings. The TransferStation will enter the Exit Control of the ParallelStation automatically once
we configure the TransferStation.
Configure the Stations Which Handle the Pallet
Plant Simulation Help 10-735
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Finally, insert and configure the TransferStation.
◦ Drag the station from which the parts arrive, the ParallelStation in our case, onto the TransferStation.
This enters the Exit Control into the ParallelStation.
◦ Drag the station on which the Target of the loading action (the pallet), the ConveyorPallet in our
case, is located onto the TransferStation. Select the station type Load and enter the position of the
sensor on the ConveyorPallet and click Apply. Adjust the position until it is located at the position
where it needs to be and click OK.
• Start the simulation and watch how the Turnplate rotates the part. You will not be able to see anything
with the default setting for the simulation speed, as the simulation runs too fast.
• To fix this, you can reduce the simulation speed in a reset method.
EventController.Speed := 60
• Start the simulation again and watch how the parts are first rotated to the right, moved to the
ParallelStation, and moved on to the TransferStation, which loads them onto the pallet. The loaded
pallet is then fed back to the Turnplate, which rotates it four times around its center to the left and them
moves it on to Shipping.
Configure the Stations Which Handle the Pallet
10-736 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model a Shrink Wrapper
Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
As we originally created the model in 2D in a previous version, we see that we have to adapt some
settings, when we run the simulation model.
• The parts look strange.
As we created the model in a previous version, it uses the size of the part of that version:
Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
Plant Simulation Help 10-737
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

In the current version the part has these dimensions:
When we enter 0.1 meters as the height of our part, MyPart, and leave length and width at 0.8 meters,
it looks much better:
Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
10-738 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
If the parts are shown with an offset on the ParallelStation and on the pallet, check the position of the
booking point of the part, compare Booking Point Length and Booking Point Width.
Booking point with an offset Booking point centered
• The pallet is still too big in 3D though. To fix this, we replace its graphic in 3D, by opening MyPallet in 3D
in the Class Library and selecting the context menu command Exchange Graphics in the 3D window.
We use this EUR-pallet:
Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
Plant Simulation Help 10-739
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• The pallet now fits the width of the conveyor, the parts are much too big, they protrude the pallet and
they overlap.
This is easy to fix though:
◦ Right-click MyPart in the Class Library and select Edit 3D Properties.
◦ Change to the tab Transformation and reduce the scaling. You might have to clear Scale
automatically to show the settings. We selected a uniform scaling factor of 0.5. This then looks
like this:
Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
10-740 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Then, we select a different color for the part in 3D to better see it in the model and adjust the positions
of the objects in the model. To prevent the parts from breaking through the glass plates at the back and
the front of the ParallelStation, we rotate it by 90 degrees.
• We finally save the model as a 3D Only model with a different name and run the simulation again.
As we'd expect the part parts are produced, conveyed, and loaded onto the pallet. The last part is
proceeding so fast though that we cannot follow the animation with our eyes.
Proceed as follows to fix this:
◦ Open the EventController and select a Real-time factor of 10.
Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
Plant Simulation Help 10-741
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

This also makes our reset method redundant and we can delete it.
◦ We then would have to wait too long for the simulation to continue with the original Time of
Creation for the pallet of 10 minutes. For this reason we shorten the Time of Creation to 1 minute.
We also adjusted the processing time of the ParallelStation. Now we can follow the simulation and
the animation with our naked eye again.
We can also increase the capacity of the pallet in the Z-Dimension from 1 to 3 to stack the parts on it.
To facilitate this we also have to triple the number of parts that the Source named SourceParts produces.
Adjust Shrinkwrapper and Parts
10-742 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model a Shrink Wrapper
Move Parts On with the Turntable
You can use the Turntable to model a rotating platform, which moves the part onto one of several
connected material flow objects.
In the sample model the Source named PartsIn produces parts of type MyPart and moves a single part
each on the feeder line of the Turntable 
 . This in turn moves the parts cyclically on to its successors, i.e.,
to the feeder lines of the Drains, which we named OutA and OutB in our model.
You can insert the Turntable into your simulation model from the folder MaterialFlow in the Class Library
or from the toolbar Material Flow in the Toolbox.
Move Parts On with the Turntable
Plant Simulation Help 10-743
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The finished simulation model looks like this:
To create the simulation model, proceed as follows:
• First, configure the Source. Enter the interval during which it produces the parts and select the part it
is to produce. We selected the negative exponential distribution, entered the parameters 0:10, 0,
1:40, and selected the part MyPart, which we created in the folder UserObjects.
Move Parts On with the Turntable
10-744 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Insert the conveyor that feeds the parts to the Turntable. You can make it as long as you like, but you do
not have to change any of the other settings.
• Insert the Turntable and its direct successors in the sequence of stations and connect them. The
Turntable needs the successors to configure its Entry Angle Table and its Exit Angle Table. In our
model these successors are the feeder lines of the Drains.
Move Parts On with the Turntable
Plant Simulation Help 10-745
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Our Turntable is 4 meters long and has its Rotation Point in the center, i.e., at 2 meters.
• To connect the Turntable with its successors, select the Connector in the Toolbox and click the end
point of the Turntable. Drag the mouse to the starting point of the Conveyor and click the left mouse
button. You’ll notice that the starting point of the Connector jumps to the left on the rotation circle of
Move Parts On with the Turntable
10-746 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

the Turntable. This is because Plant Simulation computes the angle at which the successor is located in
the Frame in relation to the Turntable.
To view the exit angles, click Exit Angles Table.
• Configure the Drains and start the simulation. Watch how the part moves onto the Turntable. It first
rotates to the line FeederPartA, and moves the part onto it. It then returns to its original position, picks
up the next part, rotates to the line FeederPartA, and moves the part onto it, and so on.
• To make the Turntable turn the part around before moving it onto the top feeder line, open the
Exit Angle Table and select Which Side > MU leaves backward for the line FeederPartA. Start the
simulation again and watch how the Turntable turns the part around.
By default the Turntable is positioned in the air in 3D if you do not define any legs for it. If you do define
legs, the entire Turntable rotates, including the legs. As this is not realistic, we create a cylindrical base on
which the Turntable proper then rotates.
• Open the Turntable in a new window.
• Click the right mouse button and select Edit 3D Properties. Change to the tab Graphics. Click Add and
create a new graphic group for the base of the Turntable. We named our graphic group base.
Click OK.
Move Parts On with the Turntable
